---
/**
 * foodcraft / AreaBL — "Kalinaw Coffee" DELIVERY & CATERING (design element
 * data-component="SERVICE-AREA").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one coverage panel. A café that runs coffee and pastry out
 * to nearby offices sells that on a plain list of places and — where the owner
 * gave one — how long each run takes. It is the only section in this template
 * drawn as a TINTED CARD sitting inside the page gutter: not a band, not the
 * cream ground, a --paper-2 panel inside a --line hairline.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *   section  padding 76px 0 (the design writes 76px 32px 0; the 32px is its own
 *            page gutter, which is .wrap here, and the 0 bottom is because its
 *            siblings are all padding-top-only)
 *   panel    background #F7EFE1 · 1px #E9DFCF ALL ROUND · radius 16px ·
 *            padding 42px 40px             → --paper-2 / --line / --r-lg
 *   head     flex · align-items flex-end · space-between · gap 26px ·
 *            flex-wrap wrap · margin-bottom 24px
 *   eyebrow  10px / .42em caps / #B4541F / 600            → .kick
 *   h2       Fraunces 400 · clamp(24px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.16 · margin .26em 0 0 ·
 *            #241A12                                      → .h2 + .sa-h2
 *   note     14.5px / 1.7 / #6B5C4E / max-width 34ch / margin 0 → --ink-soft
 *   grid     repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 32px (no ROW gap — the hairline
 *            under each row IS the separation)
 *   row      flex · baseline · gap 12px · padding 12px 0 · border-bottom 1px
 *            #E5D9C6                       → the wrapper's .rowrule, see below
 *   place    14.5px / #241A12                             → --ink
 *   time     margin-left auto · 13px / #B4541F / nowrap    → --accent
 * Not one hex is written in this file (R1).
 *
 * THE ONE HAIRLINE THAT HAS NO TOKEN, and the one decision here worth a second
 * opinion. The design's row rule is #E5D9C6 and PageBL declares no token with
 * that value: --line-2 #EDE2D1 (what .rowrule ships), --line #E9DFCF, --line-3
 * #F2EADC, --line-btn #E0D3C0. This row sits on the TINTED #F7EFE1 panel rather
 * than on paper, and the design darkens the rule for exactly that reason — the
 * hairline is the only structure this section has, so its weight is content,
 * not decoration. Nearest declared value wins: --line-btn is off by (5,6,6) per
 * channel where .rowrule's --line-2 is off by (8,9,11) and reads noticeably
 * fainter on the tinted ground. So the row keeps the wrapper's .rowrule for
 * every metric — flex, baseline, gap 12px, padding 12px 0, min-width 0, which
 * is character-for-character this design's row — and overrides the ONE property
 * the design disagrees on. Reverting is deleting one declaration.
 * (Deliberately NOT argued from "which token survives an admin scheme": --line
 * is the one of the four that buildOverrideCss remaps, and picking it on that
 * ground would be re-deriving the palette to suit the engine, which is the
 * exact move that shipped Kubo Stays looking like a different design.)
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "area" — a data-field on any other path is a dead hook that no sidebar
 * input owns)
 *   area.tag                — the eyebrow
 *   area.headline           — set:html, so an owner can put this template's
 *                             signature italic-rust <em> anywhere inside their
 *                             own sentence
 *   area.body               — the note beside the headline
 *   area.rows.${i}.place    — the primary list: place + delivery time
 *   area.rows.${i}.time
 *   area.places.${i}        — the FALLBACK list, a list of plain STRINGS. Read
 *                             only when area.rows has no usable row; rendered
 *                             place-only, with no time element emitted at all
 *                             rather than an empty rust slot
 *
 * area.body vs area.sub. area.body is the canonical path the schema owns and
 * the only one hooked. area.sub is READ (never hooked) so a site that stored a
 * paragraph there before the schema settled still shows it; the first edit
 * writes area.body, which then wins, so the two can never disagree on screen.
 * Hooking area.sub instead would emit a dead hook — v3 gates inline editing on
 * isSchemaEditablePath and the schema does not declare it.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The
 * design's own list and its "Same-day coffee and pastry runs to these areas.
 * Office catering needs a day's notice." are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 * template — a same-day promise and a notice period are operational commitments
 * for a business nobody has spoken to, so none of it ships. A row with a time
 * but no place is DROPPED: a lone rust "20 min" asserts a delivery to nowhere.
 * A row with a place and no time renders the place alone and the time element
 * is not emitted at all. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so an empty value
 * removes the ELEMENT — never a bare hairline under a row that is not there, an
 * empty rust slot, or a heading over nothing. The head is guarded at BOTH
 * levels: the whole .sa-head only exists when there is a tag, a headline or a
 * note (otherwise its 24px bottom margin opens a hole above the list), and its
 * left half only exists when there is a tag or a headline (an empty flex item
 * under space-between shoves the note to the far edge of the panel).
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes area.tag to
 * 'Service area' and, for any site with a city, area.headline to `Serving
 * ${city}` AND area.places to `[city]` — that is essentially every site we
 * ship. So the gate counts BODY content only, and discounts that one derived
 * entry:
 *   · any usable area.rows row (the schema ships area.rows EMPTY, so a filled
 *     one can only have come from a person), OR
 *   · TWO OR MORE area.places entries (the owner named a second place), OR
 *   · an owner-written area.body.
 * Counting the tag or the headline would print this panel on nearly every build
 * with a single row echoing the café's own city — the address again, dressed up
 * as a delivery zone.
 *
 * KNOWN EDGE, flagged rather than hidden: when the gate opens on area.body
 * alone and area.places is still the derived `[city]`, that one city row is
 * printed. It is true (it is where the café is) and it matches the shipped
 * behaviour of AreaBJ/AreaBK, so the catalogue stays consistent — but it is the
 * one place a derived value reaches the page, and requiring two places before
 * the list draws at all is a one-line change.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field always carries the ORIGINAL array index, so
 * dropping a blank row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2. This
 * section has no decorative numeral, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * SHAPE TOLERANCE. area.rows is a list of objects; a row that arrived as a
 * plain string is read as its place and still hooked at area.rows.${i}.place,
 * so the first edit heals the row onto the schema key instead of writing a
 * second shape.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - No id on the section and NO LINKS OF ANY KIND. The design gives
 *     SERVICE-AREA neither, and this page's ids are menu / about / gallery /
 *     location / faq / main. Because nothing here emits an href, this file
 *     carries NO onPage() anchor-remap helper and no PAGE_ANCHORS set (R7): the
 *     copy that HeaderBL / HeroBL / CtaBandBL / FooterBL share would be dead
 *     code in a file with nothing to remap. That is an omission on purpose.
 *   - No button. The design puts no action in this panel, and inventing a
 *     "Book catering" would be both fabricated copy and an anchor to an id this
 *     page does not have.
 *   - No row count asserted in markup or copy. Three tracks because the design
 *     is three tracks; four or eleven owner-typed places lay out on the same
 *     tracks without leaving a hole.
 *   - No map, no radius, no distance unit, no delivery fee. We have no such
 *     data and every one of them is a commercial claim.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). No image and no plate here, so no role="img" anywhere —
 * and nothing in this section is decorative, so nothing is aria-hidden: the
 * place and the time are both content and both announced. No element carries a
 * literal attribute beside a conditional one. The list is a real <ul> AND
 * carries role="list": the design's rows have no markers, and `list-style:
 * none` is exactly what makes Safari/VoiceOver drop list semantics, so without
 * the role the count never reaches assistive tech.
 *
 * CONTRAST (sRGB, the design's palette, measured on the --paper-2 panel rather
 * than on paper — the panel is the ground here):
 *   --ink on --paper-2 14.9 (the place) · --ink-soft on --paper-2 5.63 (note)
 *   --accent on --paper-2 4.35 — a hair BELOW AA, and the design's own value.
 *   It carries only the 13px time beside a place name set at 14.9:1, never a
 *   sentence. Swapping the time to var(--accent-deep) clears it at 6.4:1 and is
 *   a one-line change; it is the owner's palette and the owner's call, so this
 *   file ships the design's rust, the way PageBL ships the design's --muted.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped style block, no is:global. The headline is fed with
 * set:html and scoped rules cannot reach elements that set:html creates — which
 * is why there is no italic-rust rule here: PageBL styles `:where(.h2 em)` from
 * its global block, so an owner's <em> is emphasised there and a scoped
 * duplicate would be dead CSS.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const area = content.area || {};
const tag = area.tag || '';
const headline = area.headline || '';
const body = area.body || area.sub || '';

type Row = { place: string; time: string; placeField: string; timeField: string };

// PRIMARY: area.rows — place + delivery time. An object row is canonical; a
// string row is tolerated and still hooked at .place so the first edit heals it.
const rawRows: any[] = Array.isArray(area.rows) ? area.rows : [];
const timedRows: Row[] = rawRows
  .map((r: any, i: number): Row => {
    const o = r && typeof r === 'object' ? r : { place: r };
    return {
      place: String(o.place ?? '').trim(),
      time: String(o.time ?? '').trim(),
      placeField: `area.rows.${i}.place`,
      timeField: `area.rows.${i}.time`,
    };
  })
  // A time with no place is a delivery to nowhere — drop the whole row.
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

// FALLBACK: area.places — plain STRINGS. Seven templates already read them as
// strings, so nothing is reshaped here. Place-only rows; no time element.
const rawPlaces: any[] = Array.isArray(area.places) ? area.places : [];
const placeRows: Row[] = rawPlaces
  .map((p: any, i: number): Row => ({
    place: String(p ?? '').trim(),
    time: '',
    placeField: `area.places.${i}`,
    timeField: '',
  }))
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

const rows: Row[] = timedRows.length > 0 ? timedRows : placeRows;
// See SECTION GATE in the block above. Never `rows.length > 0`.
const show = timedRows.length > 0 || placeRows.length >= 2 || Boolean(body);
---
{show && (
  <section class="sa">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sa-panel">
        {(tag || headline || body) && (
          <div class="sa-head reveal">
            {(tag || headline) && (
              <div class="sa-title">
                {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="area.tag">{tag}</span>}
                {headline && <h2 class="h2 sa-h2" data-field="area.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
              </div>
            )}
            {body && <p class="sa-note" data-field="area.body">{body}</p>}
          </div>
        )}

        {rows.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="sa-list reveal" role="list" data-delay="1">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <li class="rowrule sa-row">
                <span class="sa-place" data-field={r.placeField}>{r.place}</span>
                {r.time && <span class="sa-time" data-field={r.timeField}>{r.time}</span>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Design: padding 76px 0. Its siblings are padding-top-only and PageBL gives
     every section symmetric padding, so the shared rhythm already supplies the
     trailing gap; only the 76px ceiling is taken here, kept on the wrapper's own
     7.6vw slope so it never crushes on a phone. Beats the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BL"] section` (0,1,2) at (0,2,0) with the scope attribute. */
  .sa { padding: clamp(56px, 7.6vw, 76px) 0; }

  /* Design: #F7EFE1 · 1px #E9DFCF all round · 16px · 42px 40px. The clamps
     resolve to exactly 42px / 40px at every width the design was drawn at and
     only give way below ~725px, where 40px of padding inside a 92vw wrap starts
     eating the rows themselves. */
  .sa-panel {
    background-color: var(--paper-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: clamp(26px, 5.8vw, 42px) clamp(20px, 5.4vw, 40px);
  }

  /* Design: flex · flex-end · space-between · gap 26px · wrap · mb 24px. */
  .sa-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 26px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 on both halves so a long unbroken owner string shrinks its half
     instead of widening the panel and pushing the page sideways (R8). */
  .sa-title { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .42em tracking, so a
     single unbroken word is unusually wide for its character count (R8). It is
     an inline-block, so it takes max-width:100% alongside the wrap — the same
     pair AboutBL / FaqBL / CredentialsBL put on their own .kick. */
  .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: Fraunces 400 · clamp(24px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.16 · margin .26em 0 0.
     DELIBERATELY smaller than the wrapper's .h2 (clamp(31px,4vw,54px)) and with
     the margin on the TOP rather than the bottom — the design sizes this panel
     head below a full section head and hangs it off the eyebrow. The wrapper's
     rule is a :where(), so this class wins with no !important.
     max-width: none — the design's SERVICE-AREA h2 carries NO measure cap and
     draws on ONE line at 1240px (the panel's 1096px of inner width holds the
     headline beside a 34ch note with room to spare), which the wrapper's
     `.h2 { max-width: 24ch }` would break to two. Releasing it cannot widen the
     panel: .sa-title is a flex item at min-width:0 and this rule carries
     overflow-wrap, so a long owner headline shrinks and breaks instead (R8).
     Same release, same reason, as ServicesBL / CredentialsBL / GalleryBL. */
  .sa-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 2.9vw, 36px);
    line-height: 1.16;
    margin: 0.26em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: 14.5px / 1.7 / #6B5C4E / 34ch / margin 0. Same COLOUR as the
     wrapper's .body primitive but not the same scale — .body clamps to
     15px/1.75 and sets no max-width — so the metrics are stated here and the
     colour still comes from the token. (0,2,0) with the scope attribute, so it
     beats the wrapper's (0,1,1) bare-paragraph margin reset. */
  .sa-note {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 32px. The ZERO row gap is the
     design: the hairline under each row is the separation, and a row gap would
     open a space between a rule and the row beneath it. minmax(0,1fr) rather
     than 1fr so a long place name cannot widen its track. */
  .sa-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 32px;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* Flex, baseline, 12px gap, 12px 0 padding and min-width:0 all come from the
     wrapper's .rowrule, which is character-for-character this design's row. Only
     list-item chrome and the ONE hairline value the design disagrees on are set
     here — see THE ONE HAIRLINE THAT HAS NO TOKEN in the frontmatter. */
  .sa-row {
    list-style: none;
    border-bottom-color: var(--line-btn);
  }

  .sa-place {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Design: margin-left auto · 13px · #B4541F · nowrap. No font-family: the
     design leaves it on Space Grotesk, so this is NOT .fig — .fig is the serif
     figure this template uses for prices and numerals elsewhere. */
  .sa-time {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design and they
     must not survive past 900px, where a 32px gutter leaves a place name and its
     time fighting over the same line. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .sa-list { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0 26px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .sa-list { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .sa-head { gap: 18px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    .sa-note { max-width: none; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .sa-place { font-size: 14px; }
    /* Release the design's nowrap at the one width where a long owner-typed
       time ("about 25 minutes on a good day") would push the viewport sideways.
       Above 520px the tracks are wide enough for it to hold on one line. */
    .sa-time { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; text-align: right; }
  }
</style>
